    "title": "Oil Prices Plunge and Stocks Surge After Cease-Fire Deal",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTDespite confusions about the terms of the deal, investors on Wednesday welcomed the move toward de-escalation between Iran and the United States. April 13 April 14 April 15 6,800 6,850 6,900 6,950 7,000 April 8, 2026Oil prices plummeted and stocks surged on Wednesday as investors cheered a last-minute cease-fire agreement between Iran and the United States, and largely shrugged off Iran’s claims that the deal had already been broken on Wednesday.The cease-fire deal came 90 minutes before a deadline Tuesday evening that President Trump had set for Iran to accede to his demands or risk widespread devastation. But neither side had fully agreed to the other’s requirements for lasting peace.The deal called for a two-week period when the United States would suspend strikes on Iran, and Tehran would allow vessels and tankers carrying oil, gas and other commodities to pass through the Strait of Hormuz, a vital waterway for transit of oil and gas.But a dispute emerged on Wednesday over whether continued attacks by Israel in southern Lebanon breached the cease-fire deal. Iran said they did, but Israel claimed that their offensive on Iranian-backed forces in Lebanon was not subject to the cease-fire. On Wednesday, Israel launched its heaviest wave of bombardment of Lebanon so far in the war.Still, investors were clinging to the prospect that energy shipments from the Persian Gulf would slowly resume.The price of Brent crude fell by as much as 17.2 percent earlier in the day, before retracing some of that drop. The international oil benchmark ended the day 13.3 percent lower at $94.75 per barrel. The price remained about 30 percent higher than it was before the war. $0$20$40$60$80$100 per barrel Joe Rennison writes about financial markets, a beat that ranges from chronicling the vagaries of the stock market to explaining the often-inscrutable trading decisions of Wall Street insiders.A version of this article appears in print on April 9, 2026, Section B, Page 1 of the New York edition with the headline: Truce Drives Oil Down, Stocks Up. Order Reprints | Today’s Paper | SubscribeRelated ContentAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
